truetype.library/CloseOutline                    truetype.library/CloseOutline

   NAME
	CloseOutline -- What OpenOutline made, given back.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CloseOutline(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: ?*truetype.Outline) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered, or null.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The outline's memory goes back, and compression.library is closed
	if the font was opened with it; null does nothing. The font images
	rendered from it stay: each is a whole font of its own.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The file's bytes are the caller's again, free to go.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenOutline`

   EXAMPLES
	    tb.CloseOutline(outline);
truetype.library/FontAxes                            truetype.library/FontAxes

   NAME
	FontAxes -- The axes a variable font has.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FontAxes(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, axes: ?[*]truetype.Axis, room: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.8. LVO -68.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `axes` - where they go; null to ask only how many there are.
	- `room` - how many `axes` has room for.

   RESULT
	How many axes the font has, whatever `room` is; 0 for a font that is
	not a variable one.

   BEHAVIOR
	The axes are the font's `fvar` table's, in its order, which is the
	order every other table counts them in. Each is given with its tag,
	the range of its values in its own units, the value a font opened
	without `TTA_Axes` is at, and the value this outline was opened at:
	the one asked for, cut to the range. The first `room` of them are
	written; a caller with less room than the answer asks again with
	more.

	An axis' name is a number of the font's `name` table, for
	`FontName`. An axis marked `hidden` is one the font asks not to be
	offered for choosing; it is set as any other.

	A font of more than 32 axes is read at its default instance: its
	axes are listed, each with its default as its value.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`axes` is the caller's.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenOutline`, `FontInstance`, `FontCoordinates`, `FontName`

   EXAMPLES
	    var axes: [8]sdk.truetype.Axis = undefined;
	    const count = tb.FontAxes(outline, &axes, axes.len);
	    for (axes[0..@min(count, axes.len)]) |axis| {
	        if (axis.tag == sdk.truetype.tableTag("wght")) heaviest = axis.maximum;
	    }
truetype.library/FontCoordinates              truetype.library/FontCoordinates

   NAME
	FontCoordinates -- Where an outline is on its font's axes, as the font's tables count it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FontCoordinates(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, coords: ?[*]i16, room: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.8. LVO -76.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `coords` - where they go; null to ask only how many there are.
	- `room` - how many `coords` has room for.

   RESULT
	How many axes the font has, whatever `room` is; 0 for a font that is
	not a variable one.

   BEHAVIOR
	A font's tables do not speak of an axis in its own units but in
	coordinates: -1 at the axis' least value, 0 at its default, 1 at
	its most, kept as a number of 14 bits of fraction, -16384 to 16384.
	A coordinate is the value's distance from the default as a part of
	the range on that side, bent as the font's `avar` table says for
	the axis, and rounded. The first `room` of them are written, in the
	axes' order.

	These are what a table of the font that varies is read with: the
	positions and the choice of features a shaper takes from `GPOS`,
	`GSUB` and `GDEF`, got through `FontTable`. The outlines, advances
	and measures this library gives are already at them.

	Every coordinate is 0 for an outline at its font's default, and for
	a font of more than 32 axes, which is read at its default.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`coords` is the caller's.

   SEE ALSO
	`FontAxes`, `OpenOutline`, `FontTable`

   EXAMPLES
	    var coords: [32]i16 = undefined;
	    const axes = tb.FontCoordinates(outline, &coords, coords.len);
	    const at_default = for (coords[0..@min(axes, coords.len)]) |coord| {
	        if (coord != 0) break false;
	    } else true;
truetype.library/FontInstance                    truetype.library/FontInstance

   NAME
	FontInstance -- One of the places on its axes a variable font names.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FontInstance(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, number: u32, values: ?[*]truetype.AxisValue, room: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.8. LVO -72.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `number` - which instance, counted from 0.
	- `values` - where its value on each axis goes; null for its name
	  only.
	- `room` - how many `values` has room for.

   RESULT
	The instance's name - "Semibold" - as the number `FontName` takes; 0
	when the font has no instance of that number, and nothing is written
	then.

   BEHAVIOR
	A variable font's designer names some places on its axes, the faces
	the family would have as separate files: Light, Regular, Bold. They
	are the `fvar` table's instances, in its order. The values are
	written an axis each, in the axes' order, with the axis' tag: the
	first `room` of them, of as many as `FontAxes` answers. They are in
	the form `TTA_Axes` takes, so the instance is opened by handing them
	to `OpenOutline`.

	Which instance an outline was opened at does not matter: every
	outline of a font answers the same.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`values` is the caller's.

   NOTES
	The numbers have no gap: a caller lists a font's instances by
	counting up from 0 until the answer is 0. A name number is never 0
	for an instance, 0 being the font's copyright; a file that says so
	all the same has its instances end there.

   SEE ALSO
	`FontAxes`, `OpenOutline`, `FontName`

   EXAMPLES
	    // Every instance the font names, opened in turn.
	    var values: [8]sdk.truetype.AxisValue = undefined;
	    var number: u32 = 0;
	    while (tb.FontInstance(outline, number, &values, values.len) != 0) : (number += 1) {
	        const tags = [_]utility.TagItem{
	            .{ .tag = sdk.truetype.TTA_Axes, .data = @intFromPtr(&values) },
	            .{ .tag = sdk.truetype.TTA_AxisCount, .data = @min(tb.FontAxes(outline, null, 0), values.len) },
	            .{ .tag = utility.TAG_DONE, .data = 0 },
	        };
	        const face = tb.OpenOutline(file, size, &tags) orelse continue;
	        defer tb.CloseOutline(face);
	    }
truetype.library/FontMetrics                      truetype.library/FontMetrics

   NAME
	FontMetrics -- A font's measures.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FontMetrics(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, metrics: *truetype.FontMetrics) void

   SINCE
	1.2, fonts of cubic curves since 1.6. LVO -44.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `metrics` - where they go.

   RESULT
	Nothing; `metrics` is filled in, in the font's units, y up.

   BEHAVIOR
	The units per em and the number of glyphs; the ascender, descender
	and line gap of the horizontal header (`hhea`), which is what
	`RenderFontImage` sets its lines by; the x and capital heights and
	the strike-out of `OS/2`, the underline of `post`, each 0 where the
	font does not give it; the most verbs and points `GlyphSegments`
	gives for a glyph of the font, from what `maxp` says the largest
	glyph is (or as for 1024 points and 128 contours, when it does not
	say); and the styles the file says it is drawn in.

	A font of cubic curves does not say how large its largest glyph is:
	`most_verbs` and `most_points` are 1283 and 2179 for it, as for 1024
	points and 128 contours, and a glyph may take more. `GlyphSegments`
	then answers `TRUETYPEERR_ROOM` with what the glyph needs.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`metrics` is the caller's.

   BUGS
	A font whose `maxp` says less than its largest glyph has is not
	read so far: that glyph's `GlyphSegments` answers
	`TRUETYPEERR_GLYPH`.

   SEE ALSO
	`GlyphMetrics`, `GlyphSegments`, `OpenOutline`

   EXAMPLES
	    var fm: sdk.truetype.FontMetrics = .{};
	    tb.FontMetrics(outline, &fm);
	    const scale = size / @as(f32, @floatFromInt(fm.units_per_em));
truetype.library/FontName                            truetype.library/FontName

   NAME
	FontName -- One of a font's names.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FontName(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, name: u32, buffer: [*]u8, room: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -56.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `name` - which name: `TTNAME_FAMILY`, `TTNAME_STYLE`, `TTNAME_FULL`,
	  `TTNAME_VERSION`, `TTNAME_LICENCE`, or any other number of the
	  font's `name` table.
	- `buffer` - where it goes, in UTF-8, NUL-terminated.
	- `room` - the buffer's size in bytes, the NUL counted; 0 to ask only
	  how long the name is, and `buffer` is then not written.

   RESULT
	The bytes the whole name takes in UTF-8, the NUL not counted: `room`
	or more says the buffer holds only the name's start. 0 when the font
	has no such name, and the buffer then holds an empty string.

   BEHAVIOR
	The name is taken in English where the font has it: of the table's
	records of that number the one in American English, then one in
	another English, then one of no language (the Unicode platform's),
	then the first in any other language. A record of no bytes counts as
	none.

	The name is stored as UTF-16 and written as UTF-8: two surrogates
	that are a pair become their one character, one without the other
	U+FFFD, and a NUL within the name is left out. A name longer than the
	buffer is cut after a whole character.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The buffer is the caller's.

   BUGS
	A name the font has only in a Macintosh encoding is not read, and a
	name cannot be asked for in a language of the caller's choice.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenOutline`, `FontMetrics`

   EXAMPLES
	    var family: [64]u8 = undefined;
	    if (tb.FontName(outline, sdk.truetype.TTNAME_FAMILY, &family, family.len) == 0) return;
	    const text: [*:0]const u8 = @ptrCast(&family);
truetype.library/FontTable                          truetype.library/FontTable

   NAME
	FontTable -- One of a font's tables, as the file has it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FontTable(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, tag: u32, length: *u32) ?[*]const u8

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -60.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `tag` - the table's four letters, as `truetype.tableTag` makes
	  them: `tableTag("GSUB")`.
	- `length` - where the table's size in bytes goes.

   RESULT
	The table's first byte, and its size in `length`. Null, and a size of
	0, when the font has no table of that tag or the file ends before the
	table does.

   BEHAVIOR
	The font's directory is searched for the tag - in a collection, the
	directory of the font `OpenOutline` was asked for - and the table's
	place and size are checked against the file. Nothing within the table
	is: what it says is its reader's to check against `length`. Its
	numbers are stored with the highest byte first.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The bytes are the file's, which is the caller's: they are there as
	long as the file is, and are not to be written.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenOutline`, `FontName`

   EXAMPLES
	    var length: u32 = 0;
	    const start = tb.FontTable(outline, sdk.truetype.tableTag("GSUB"), &length) orelse return;
	    const substitutions = start[0..length];
truetype.library/GlyphAdvance                    truetype.library/GlyphAdvance

   NAME
	GlyphAdvance -- How far a glyph moves the point.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GlyphAdvance(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, glyph: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.8, variable fonts of cubic curves since 1.9. LVO -80.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `glyph` - the glyph's number, from `GlyphIndex`.

   RESULT
	The glyph's advance in the font's units, never less than 0; 0 for a
	number past the font's glyphs.

   BEHAVIOR
	The advance is the font's horizontal metrics' (`hmtx`), the one
	`GlyphMetrics` gives. For a variable font away from its default it
	is that instance's: the advance plus what the font's `HVAR` table
	says it changes by there, rounded to a whole unit; or, in a font
	without that table, the distance between the glyph's two sides as
	its deltas in `gvar` move them, rounded. A font of cubic curves
	without `HVAR` has nothing that moves its advances: they are the
	same at every instance.

	The call reads no outline and takes no memory, which the box of a
	glyph away from the default does. It is the one to ask when a line
	of text is measured.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands.

   BUGS
	No vertical advance: text set down the page is not read.

   SEE ALSO
	`GlyphMetrics`, `GlyphIndex`, `Kerning`

   EXAMPLES
	    var width: i32 = 0;
	    for (text) |code| width += tb.GlyphAdvance(outline, tb.GlyphIndex(outline, code));
truetype.library/GlyphIndex                        truetype.library/GlyphIndex

   NAME
	GlyphIndex -- The glyph a character is.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GlyphIndex(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, code: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.2, every format of character map since 1.4. LVO -32.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `code` - the character, a Unicode code point.

   RESULT
	The glyph's number, for `GlyphSegments`, `GlyphMetrics` and
	`Kerning`; 0 - the missing glyph, which a font draws as a box or
	nothing - when the font has none for it.

   BEHAVIOR
	Looked up in the character map `OpenOutline` chose: the font's
	Unicode one that reaches furthest (formats 12, 13, 10, 4, 6 and 0, in
	that order; one of the basic plane has no glyph past U+FFFF), or a
	symbol font's own, whose characters are where the font puts them. A
	glyph the map names and the font does not have answers 0.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands.

   BUGS
	One character is one glyph: a font's substitutions - ligatures, the
	forms of a script's letters by where they are in a word - are not
	made. A character map of format 2 or 8 is not read.

   SEE ALSO
	`GlyphVariant`, `GlyphSegments`, `GlyphMetrics`, `Kerning`

   EXAMPLES
	    const glyph = tb.GlyphIndex(outline, 'A');
truetype.library/GlyphMetrics                    truetype.library/GlyphMetrics

   NAME
	GlyphMetrics -- A glyph's advance, left side bearing and box.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GlyphMetrics(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, glyph: u32, metrics: *truetype.GlyphMetrics) bool

   SINCE
	1.2, fonts of cubic curves since 1.6, fonts of pictures since
	1.7, variable fonts since 1.8, those of cubic curves since 1.9.
	LVO -36.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `glyph` - the glyph's number, from `GlyphIndex`.
	- `metrics` - where they go.

   RESULT
	True, and `metrics` filled in, in the font's units, y up; false - and
	`metrics` all 0 - for a number past the font's glyphs, and for a
	glyph of a variable font away from its default when there is not the
	memory to read it.

   BEHAVIOR
	The advance and the left side bearing are the font's horizontal
	metrics (`hmtx`); the box is the one the glyph's data gives, which
	holds its points on the curve and off it. A glyph of no outline, a
	space, has a box of 0s and its advance.

	A glyph of cubic curves has no box in its data: its program is run
	and the box is the one of its points, the curves' control points
	among them, widened to whole units. A glyph whose program is damaged
	has a box of 0s and its advance.

	A glyph that is a picture has the box of its largest picture: the
	picture's measures in its strike's pixels, brought to the font's
	units by the strike's pixels an em and widened to whole units. Its
	advance and left side bearing are the font's horizontal metrics, as
	any glyph's. A glyph no strike has a picture of has a box of 0s.

	A `glyf` glyph of a variable font away from its default has the
	measures of that instance. The box its data gives is the default's,
	so the glyph is read with its deltas, in memory taken and given back
	within the call, and the box is the one of its points there, the
	curves' control points among them, widened to whole units. The
	advance is `GlyphAdvance`'s. The left side bearing is from the
	glyph's origin there - its left side point as the deltas move it,
	rounded - to the left of that box. A glyph whose deltas are damaged
	has a box of 0s and its advance.

	A glyph of cubic curves of a variable font away from its default
	(`CFF2`) has its deltas in its program, so its box is that
	instance's as it is at the default: the program is run, and no
	memory is taken. Its origin is where its program starts from, at
	every instance, and its left side bearing is the left of its box
	there, 0 for a glyph of no outline.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`metrics` is the caller's.

   NOTES
	A caller that wants the advance only asks `GlyphAdvance`, which
	takes no memory at any instance.

   BUGS
	No vertical metrics: text set down the page is not read.

   SEE ALSO
	`GlyphIndex`, `GlyphAdvance`, `FontMetrics`, `GlyphSegments`

   EXAMPLES
	    var m: sdk.truetype.GlyphMetrics = .{};
	    if (tb.GlyphMetrics(outline, glyph, &m)) x += m.advance;
truetype.library/GlyphSegments                  truetype.library/GlyphSegments

   NAME
	GlyphSegments -- A glyph's outline as lines and curves.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GlyphSegments(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, glyph: u32, segments: *truetype.Segments) i32

   SINCE
	1.2, `SEG_CUBIC` of a font of cubic curves since 1.6. LVO -40.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `glyph` - the glyph's number, from `GlyphIndex`.
	- `segments` - the caller's arrays of verbs and points, and how many
	  each holds; the counts are set.

   RESULT
	- `TRUETYPE_OK` - the outline is in the arrays, `verb_count` verbs
	  and `point_count` points; none for a glyph of no outline, a space.
	- `TRUETYPEERR_ROOM` - it did not fit: the counts say how many it
	  needs, and what is in the arrays is not to be used.
	- `TRUETYPEERR_GLYPH` - no glyph of that number, or its data is
	  damaged; the counts are 0.
	- `TRUETYPEERR_NOMEM` - no memory to read it in.

   BEHAVIOR
	The verbs are vector.library's - `SEG_MOVE`, `SEG_LINE`, `SEG_QUAD`
	or, in a font of cubic curves, `SEG_CUBIC`, and `SEG_CLOSE` - and the
	points `vector.Point`s, so the arrays go to `AddSegments` as they are. They are in the font's units, y up, from
	the glyph's origin on the baseline: a program drawing them scales by
	its size over `FontMetrics`' units per em and turns y over. Each
	contour is closed, and filled by the non-zero rule its contours are
	drawn for. A composite glyph is its parts, each moved and scaled as
	the font says, in one outline.

	A glyph of cubic curves comes out as its program draws it: a move
	to where a contour begins, a line or a curve for each one drawn - a
	line drawn back to the beginning among them - and the close. A
	contour of nothing but its first point is left out. The subroutines
	a program calls are run, its hints passed over.

	`FontMetrics`' `most_verbs` and `most_points` are long enough for any
	glyph of a font of quadratic curves. A font of cubic curves does not
	say how large its glyphs are: one larger than that answers
	`TRUETYPEERR_ROOM` and is read into arrays of the counts it gives.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The arrays are the caller's. The call allocates room to read the
	glyph in, and gives it back before it returns.

   BUGS
	No hinting: the outline is as it was drawn, for any size. A part of a
	composite placed by matching its points to another's is left where it
	was drawn. A glyph of cubic curves made of two others (an `endchar`
	with four numbers) has no outline.

   SEE ALSO
	`GlyphIndex`, `GlyphMetrics`, `FontMetrics`,
	`vector.library/AddSegments`

   EXAMPLES
	    var s = sdk.truetype.Segments{ .verbs = &verbs, .points = &points, .verb_room = verbs.len, .point_room = points.len };
	    if (tb.GlyphSegments(outline, glyph, &s) == sdk.truetype.TRUETYPE_OK) {
	        _ = vb.AddSegments(path, s.verbs, s.verb_count, s.points, s.point_count);
	    }
truetype.library/GlyphVariant                    truetype.library/GlyphVariant

   NAME
	GlyphVariant -- The glyph a character and a variation selector are.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GlyphVariant(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, code: u32, selector: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -52.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `code` - the character, a Unicode code point.
	- `selector` - the variation selector that follows it: U+FE00 to
	  U+FE0F, U+E0100 to U+E01EF, or U+180B to U+180D.

   RESULT
	The number of the glyph the two are drawn as; 0 when the font has no
	such sequence, and the character is then drawn as `GlyphIndex` says,
	the selector left out.

   BEHAVIOR
	Looked up in the font's variation sequences (the character map's
	format 14). A sequence the font has a glyph of its own for answers
	that glyph: U+FE0F after a symbol its picture in colour, U+FE0E its
	form as text, a selector after an ideograph one of its registered
	shapes. A sequence the font lists as drawn the way the character is
	without a selector answers the character's own glyph.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`GlyphIndex`, `GlyphMetrics`

   EXAMPLES
	    // A heart, asked for in colour.
	    var glyph = tb.GlyphVariant(outline, 0x2764, 0xFE0F);
	    if (glyph == 0) glyph = tb.GlyphIndex(outline, 0x2764);
truetype.library/Kerning                              truetype.library/Kerning

   NAME
	Kerning -- How much further apart two glyphs are drawn side by side.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn Kerning(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, left: u32, right: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.2. LVO -48.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `left` - the glyph drawn first, from `GlyphIndex`.
	- `right` - the one after it.

   RESULT
	The font's units added to `left`'s advance before `right` is drawn:
	negative to draw them closer, as "AV" is. 0 for a pair the font does
	not move.

   BEHAVIOR
	The pairs of the font's `kern` table: those of its subtables of
	format 0 for horizontal text, summed - one that says it overrides
	taking the place of what came before it.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands.

   BUGS
	Only the `kern` table is read, not OpenType's `GPOS`: a font that
	keeps its kerning only there is drawn without it. Apple's version of
	the table is not read either.

   SEE ALSO
	`GlyphIndex`, `GlyphMetrics`

   EXAMPLES
	    x += m.advance + tb.Kerning(outline, glyph, next);
truetype.library/OpenOutline                      truetype.library/OpenOutline

   NAME
	OpenOutline -- A font of a TrueType or OpenType file, opened for rendering.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn OpenOutline(tb: *TrueTypeBase, file: [*]const u8, size: u32, tags: ?[*]const utility.TagItem) ?*truetype.Outline

   SINCE
	1.0, the tags and collections since 1.4, fonts of cubic curves
	since 1.6, fonts of pictures since 1.7, variable fonts since 1.8,
	those of cubic curves since 1.9, `TTA_Plain` since 1.10. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	- `file` - the whole file, as read.
	- `size` - its size in bytes.
	- `tags` - which font of the file is meant and at which place on
	  its axes, or null for its first at its default:
	  - `TTA_FontIndex` (u32) - the font's number in a collection, from 0;
	    `truetype.fontCount` says how many the file holds. Default 0.
	  - `TTA_Axes` ([*]const truetype.AxisValue) - where on a variable
	    font's axes: an axis by its tag and a value in the axis' own
	    units, as `FontAxes` gives its range. Default: every axis at
	    its default.
	  - `TTA_AxisCount` (u32) - how many values `TTA_Axes` points at.
	    Default 0.
	  - `TTA_Plain` (bool) - render the outline as it is drawn, no
	    height fitted to the rows. Default false.

   RESULT
	The outline, or null if the file has no font of that number, the
	bytes are not a font this library reads - a table it needs missing,
	outside the file or not sound, no character map it reads - there is
	no memory, or the font's pictures are PNG files and
	compression.library does not open.

   BEHAVIOR
	The font's tables are found and checked once, and what the other
	calls need of them kept: the units per em, the ascender and
	descender, where the glyph index, the glyphs, the widths, the
	character map and the variation sequences are. The glyphs themselves
	are read only when one is asked for.

	Of a font's character maps a Unicode one is read, the one that
	reaches furthest: formats 12, 13, 10, 4, 6 and 0, in that order. A
	symbol font, which has none, is read by its own map, with its
	characters where the font puts them (U+F020 to U+F0FF mostly).

	A font's glyphs are of lines and quadratic curves (`glyf` and
	`loca`), or of lines and cubic curves (`CFF `, or a variable font's
	`CFF2`, in a file that starts 'OTTO'), a font keyed by character
	collection among those. A font with both is read by `glyf`, and one
	with both tables of cubic curves by `CFF `. Of such a table the
	lists, the dictionaries and where they put the glyphs' programs, the
	subroutines, each glyph's font dictionary and a `CFF2` table's store
	of deltas are checked here, and a table that is not sound opens no
	font; a glyph's program is run when the glyph is asked for.

	A font need not have outlines. One without them whose glyphs are
	pictures is rendered from those: colour pictures, PNG files in
	strikes of 32 bits a pixel (`CBLC` and `CBDT`, a picture's formats
	17, 18 and 19), or pictures of coverage in strikes of 1, 2, 4 or 8
	bits a pixel (`EBLC` and `EBDT`, formats 1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9). A
	font with both pairs of tables is read by the colour one; the first
	64 strikes of a list are read, and a list of another version than 2
	or 3 is none. For a font of colour pictures compression.library is
	opened, which unpacks them, and held until `CloseOutline`. Such a
	font maps, measures and names as any other, and every glyph of it is
	one of no outline (`GlyphSegments`). A font with outlines is drawn
	from them at every size, and its pictures are not read.

	A variable font (`fvar`) is opened at one place on its axes, its
	instance, and reads as a font of that face. An axis `TTA_Axes` does
	not name is at its default, a value outside an axis' range is cut to
	it, a tag the font has no axis of is passed over, and of two values
	for one axis the first counts. Each value becomes a coordinate - its
	distance from the default as a part of the range on that side, bent
	by the font's `avar` table, in 14 bits of fraction - and at those
	coordinates the font is read from then on: a `glyf` glyph's points
	with their deltas (`gvar`), a `CFF2` glyph's program with the deltas
	that follow its numbers (each number and, for every region of the
	table's store, its delta as far as the region counts at the
	coordinates), the advances with theirs (`HVAR`, or `gvar`'s side
	points in a font without it; a `CFF2` font without `HVAR` has the
	same advances at every instance), and the x height, the cap height,
	the underline and the strikeout with theirs (`MVAR`).
	The ascender, the descender and the line gap are the same at every
	instance. A font opened at its default, and one without axes, reads
	none of these tables. A table of deltas that is not for the font's
	number of axes is passed over, and the font read as at its default
	in what that table holds. A font of more than 32 axes is opened at
	its default whatever the tags say.

	Three heights are kept for rendering, which fits them to the rows of
	a size (`RenderFontImage`): the baseline, the x height and the cap
	height. A font that does not say one of the two heights - an `OS/2`
	table before version 2, or none - has the top of its `x` or of its
	`H` taken for it, and a font without that letter has no such height
	fitted. A variable font of quadratic curves opened away from its
	default takes no letter's top: only what the font says is fitted.
	`FontMetrics` answers what the font says, whatever is fitted. With
	`TTA_Plain` nothing is fitted, and what is rendered is the outline
	scaled and no more.

	The outline is not written after this call, and no other call keeps
	anything in it: any number of tasks use one outline at once.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: for a font of colour pictures, while compression.library is
	  loaded, if it is not in memory yet; otherwise no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do, but for a font of colour pictures while
	  compression.library is still on the disk: loading it takes a
	  Process.

   OWNERSHIP
	The outline is the caller's until `CloseOutline`. The file's bytes stay
	the caller's too, and must be there, unchanged, until then: the
	outline reads them in place. The tags are read during the call only.

   BUGS
	Hinting instructions are passed over, and so are a `CFF ` table's
	hints and its font matrix: its glyphs are taken as drawn in the
	font's units. A `CFF2` table's hints are passed over the same way,
	and with them the deltas its private dictionaries have for them. An
	`avar` table of version 2, which bends one axis by the others, is
	passed over. A font whose only character map is in a Macintosh
	encoding is not opened.

	Pictures in `sbix`, and in the `bloc` and `bdat` tables, are not
	read, nor are a picture's formats 3 and 4, nor the measures of a
	line set downwards.

   SEE ALSO
	`RenderFontImage`, `GlyphIndex`, `FontName`, `FontTable`, `FontAxes`,
	`FontInstance`, `CloseOutline`

   EXAMPLES
	    const outline = tb.OpenOutline(file, size, null) orelse return error.NotTrueType;
	    defer tb.CloseOutline(outline);

	    // Every font of a collection.
	    var index: u32 = 0;
	    while (index < sdk.truetype.fontCount(file[0..size])) : (index += 1) {
	        const tags = [_]utility.TagItem{
	            .{ .tag = sdk.truetype.TTA_FontIndex, .data = index },
	            .{ .tag = utility.TAG_DONE, .data = 0 },
	        };
	        const font = tb.OpenOutline(file, size, &tags) orelse continue;
	        defer tb.CloseOutline(font);
	    }

	    // A variable font at a weight of 650.
	    const place = [_]sdk.truetype.AxisValue{.{ .tag = sdk.truetype.tableTag("wght"), .value = 650 }};
	    const at = [_]utility.TagItem{
	        .{ .tag = sdk.truetype.TTA_Axes, .data = @intFromPtr(&place) },
	        .{ .tag = sdk.truetype.TTA_AxisCount, .data = place.len },
	        .{ .tag = utility.TAG_DONE, .data = 0 },
	    };
	    const heavy = tb.OpenOutline(file, size, &at) orelse return error.NotTrueType;
	    defer tb.CloseOutline(heavy);
truetype.library/RenderFontImage              truetype.library/RenderFontImage

   NAME
	RenderFontImage -- One size of an outline, as a font image.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RenderFontImage(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, rows: u32, ranges: [*]const truetype.CodeRange, count: u32) ?*FontImage

   SINCE
	1.0, ranges since 1.3, the limits since 1.4, fonts of cubic
	curves since 1.6, fonts of pictures since 1.7, heights fitted to the
	rows since 1.10. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `rows` - how tall a line is, 1 to 4096: the font's ascender to its
	  descender is scaled to this.
	- `ranges` - the characters to render, as code points: `count` ranges,
	  sorted, none overlapping another.
	- `count` - how many ranges.

   RESULT
	A font image of coverage (`alpha4`), or of colour (`rgba32`) for a
	font of colour pictures, sound and ready for `AddFont`: every
	character of the ranges the font has a glyph for, and the font's
	missing glyph as the default character, under `fontimage.MISSING`.
	Null for a height of 0 or more than 4096, a font with neither
	outlines nor pictures, ranges out of order or past the last code
	point, an image of more than a gigabyte, or no memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	A character the font's character map gives no glyph is left out, so
	that graphics draws it as its base letter where the image has that
	(`Ā` as `A`) and as the missing glyph otherwise; the image's own
	ranges are the runs of characters it has.

	Every glyph is its outline scaled so that the line is `rows` tall,
	the curves - quadratic or cubic - made lines within a third of a
	pixel, and each pixel given how much of it the outline covers, in
	fifteenths.

	Three heights of the font are fitted to the rows, so that the feet
	and heads of a line of text are whole rows and not rows half
	covered. The baseline is on the edge of the row nearest to the
	ascender's share of the line - the row the image names as its
	baseline. The x height is the nearest whole number of rows above it,
	one at least, and the cap height the nearest whole number that is
	above the x height's. A point between two of these heights keeps its
	share of the way from one to the other; one below the baseline or
	above the cap height keeps its distance from it. So a glyph is moved
	up or down by half a row at most, and nothing moves sideways: the
	columns and the advances are those of the outline scaled. A height
	the font has not (`OpenOutline`) is not fitted. An outline opened
	with `TTA_Plain` is scaled and no more: its baseline is where the
	ascender's share of the line falls, and the image names the row
	nearest to it.

	A glyph of a font whose glyphs are pictures is its picture scaled to
	the line, as `RenderGlyph` says: at a strike's own size, pixel for
	pixel.
	The advances are rounded to whole pixels. The font is `FPF_DESIGNED`
	- drawn at its size from the font's file, not scaled from another
	image - and `FPF_PROPORTIONAL` when its advances differ. Its styles are the ones the font says it is drawn
	in (`FontMetrics`): a bold face renders as `FSF_BOLD`, so graphics
	draws no bold over it.

	A glyph the file is damaged in - its data cut short, a program of
	cubic curves that cannot be run, its outline further than sixteen
	lines from where it is drawn - is rendered as one of no outline, with
	its advance; an advance is 32767 pixels at most.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no. The work is a glyph rendered for each character the
	  font has in the ranges: hundreds for a European font's scripts.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The image is the caller's, one allocation, freed with `FreeVec`.

   BUGS
	Only heights are fitted, and only those three: an upright stem at a
	small size may fall between two columns and show as both half
	covered, and a flat stroke at another height - the bar of an `e` -
	between two rows. A font's own hinting program is not run.

	The pictures of a font that has outlines too are not drawn, at any
	size.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenOutline`, `RenderGlyph`, `graphics.library/AddFont`, `diskfont.library/OpenDiskFont`

   EXAMPLES
	    const latin1 = [_]truetype.CodeRange{ .{ .first = 0x20, .last = 0x7E }, .{ .first = 0xA0, .last = 0xFF } };
	    const image = tb.RenderFontImage(outline, 24, &latin1, latin1.len) orelse return;
	    defer sys.FreeVec(image);
truetype.library/RenderGlyph                      truetype.library/RenderGlyph

   NAME
	RenderGlyph -- One glyph of an outline, by its number, as a font image has a glyph.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RenderGlyph(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, glyph: u32, rows: u32, image: *truetype.GlyphImage) i32

   SINCE
	1.5, fonts of cubic curves since 1.6, fonts of pictures since
	1.7, heights fitted to the rows since 1.10. LVO -64.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `glyph` - the glyph's number: from `GlyphIndex`, `GlyphVariant`, or
	  a shaped run.
	- `rows` - how tall a line is, 1 to 4096: the font's ascender to its
	  descender is scaled to this.
	- `image` - the caller's bytes for the pixels and how many there are;
	  the rest of it is set.

   RESULT
	- `TRUETYPE_OK` - the glyph is in `image`: its box and advance in
	  `glyph`, `size` bytes of pixels written. A glyph with no ink - a
	  space - has a box of nothing, no bytes, and its advance.
	- `TRUETYPEERR_ROOM` - the pixels did not fit: `size` says how many
	  bytes they take and `glyph` is set, and nothing is written.
	- `TRUETYPEERR_GLYPH` - no glyph of that number, or a font with
	  neither outlines nor pictures.
	- `TRUETYPEERR_ROWS` - a height of 0 or more than 4096.
	- `TRUETYPEERR_NOMEM` - no memory to render it in.

	With every answer but the first two, `size` is 0 and `glyph` is a
	glyph of nothing. `kind` is set with every answer.

   BEHAVIOR
	The glyph is rendered as `RenderFontImage` renders the glyphs of a
	font image `rows` tall, and comes out the same byte for byte: its
	outline scaled so that the line is `rows` tall, the font's baseline,
	x height and cap height fitted to the rows unless the outline was
	opened with `TTA_Plain`, each pixel given how much of it the outline
	covers, in fifteenths. `image.kind` says what
	the bytes are - `alpha4`, two pixels a byte, the left one in the high
	four bits, or `rgba32` for a font of colour pictures, a pixel's red,
	green, blue and coverage a byte each - and `image.glyph` is a font
	image's glyph: the box of its ink, `left` columns from the point and
	`top` rows down from the top of the line, the bytes from one row to
	the next, and the advance in whole pixels. Its `data` is 0.

	A font without outlines whose glyphs are pictures (`CBLC` and `CBDT`,
	or `EBLC` and `EBDT`) is rendered from them. Of the strikes that have
	a picture of the glyph the one is taken whose em is the fewest pixels
	no smaller than the line's, or the largest when all are smaller. The
	picture is scaled by the line's em over the strike's, its baseline on
	a whole row, and each pixel of the glyph is the mean of the picture
	over that pixel's area, colour weighted by coverage: at the strike's
	own size the picture comes out pixel for pixel. The glyph's box is
	the scaled picture's, not cut to its ink, so the bytes a glyph takes
	follow from its measures, and `TRUETYPEERR_ROOM` is answered without
	the picture being read. A picture that cannot be read - its data too
	short, a PNG file that is damaged or not the size its measures say,
	parts nested more than four deep - is its box with nothing in it. A
	glyph no strike has a picture of is one of no ink, with its advance.

	A glyph the file is damaged in - its data cut short, a program of
	cubic curves that cannot be run, its outline further than sixteen
	lines from where it is drawn - is one of no ink, with its advance; an
	advance is 32767 pixels at most.

	A glyph of cubic curves is rendered whatever its size: one of more
	points than `FontMetrics` counts on is read into the room it needs.

	The outline is only read, and what the call works in is its own: any
	number of tasks render from one outline at once.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`image` and its bytes are the caller's. The call allocates the room
	it renders in and gives it back before it returns.

   NOTES
	An answer of `TRUETYPEERR_ROOM` has cost an outline's rendering: a
	caller that keeps room for the glyphs it mostly draws, and asks again
	with more for the few that are larger, renders those twice.

	A picture is not sharpened: one drawn larger than its strike is its
	pixels spread over their areas.

   BUGS
	Only heights are fitted, and only those three: an upright stem at a
	small size may fall between two columns and show as both half
	covered, and a flat stroke at another height - the bar of an `e` -
	between two rows. A font's own hinting program is not run.

	The pictures of a font that has outlines too are not drawn, at any
	size.

   SEE ALSO
	`RenderFontImage`, `GlyphIndex`, `GlyphMetrics`,
	`truetypeshaping.library/ShapeText`

   EXAMPLES
	    var bytes: [1024]u8 = undefined;
	    var image = sdk.truetype.GlyphImage{ .pixels = &bytes, .room = bytes.len };
	    if (tb.RenderGlyph(outline, glyph, 24, &image) == sdk.truetype.TRUETYPE_OK) {
	        // image.glyph.width by image.glyph.rows pixels in bytes[0..image.size].
	    }
truetype.library/VariationDelta                truetype.library/VariationDelta

   NAME
	VariationDelta -- What an item of a store of deltas adds at the outline's place.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn VariationDelta(tb: *TrueTypeBase, outline: *truetype.Outline, store: [*]const u8, size: u32, item: u32, regions: ?*u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.11. LVO -84.

   INPUTS
	- `outline` - what `OpenOutline` answered.
	- `store` - an item variation store: the bytes of one, wherever
	  they are.
	- `size` - how many bytes the store has; a store that is the rest of
	  a table has the bytes to the table's end.
	- `item` - the item asked for: the number of its data in the high 16
	  bits, of its row there in the low 16. `truetype.variationItem`
	  puts the two together.
	- `regions` - where the number of deltas the row has goes, one a
	  region: how much the call read. Null for a caller that does not
	  count it.

   RESULT
	What the item adds to its number at the place the outline was
	opened at, in the font's units, rounded to a whole unit, a half
	upward. 0 at the font's default and for a font without axes, for an
	item the store does not have, and for a store that is not for the
	font's number of axes.

   BEHAVIOR
	A variable font's tables hold their numbers for its default place.
	A number that is another one elsewhere - an advance, a measure, a
	kerning, an anchor - names an item of a store of deltas, and the
	item says by how much the number changes towards each of the store's
	regions. A region is a stretch of each axis with a peak in it: at
	the peak its delta counts in full, at the stretch's ends and outside
	it not at all, in between in proportion; the parts of a region's
	axes are multiplied. An axis whose peak is 0 takes no part in the
	region, and one whose three numbers are out of order, or lie on both
	sides of 0, counts in full.

	The row's deltas, each times its region's part, are summed in the
	order they are stored, in 64-bit floating point, and the sum is
	rounded once. It is the rule the library varies a glyph's advance
	and the font's measures by, with the same code: one store's item is
	one number wherever it is asked for, on every machine.

	The store is read within its `size` bytes and nowhere else: a count
	is cut to what the bytes hold, an offset of 0 or past them names
	nothing, a row that does not lie within them adds 0, and a delta for
	a region the store's list does not hold is left out. The answer is
	held to 30 bits either way.

	`regions` gets 0 where no row was read - at the default, for an item
	that is not there. A caller that bounds its work by what a font can
	make it read takes the number off its count.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Nothing changes hands. The store's bytes are the caller's and are
	only read.

   NOTES
	The stores of the font's own tables need no call: `GlyphAdvance` and
	`FontMetrics` answer at the outline's place already. The call is for
	the tables the library hands out with `FontTable` and does not read
	itself - `GDEF`'s store, which `GPOS`'s value records and anchors
	name their items in.

   BUGS
	A store whose regions are for another number of axes than the font
	has adds 0 for every item, also where the two share their first
	axes.

   SEE ALSO
	`FontTable`, `FontCoordinates`, `GlyphAdvance`, `OpenOutline`

   EXAMPLES
	    // GDEF 1.3 has the offset of its store at byte 14.
	    var length: u32 = 0;
	    const gdef = tb.FontTable(outline, truetype.tableTag("GDEF"), &length) orelse return;
	    if (length < 18) return;
	    const at = @as(u32, gdef[14]) << 24 | @as(u32, gdef[15]) << 16 | @as(u32, gdef[16]) << 8 | gdef[17];
	    if (at == 0 or at >= length) return;
	    const more = tb.VariationDelta(outline, gdef + at, length - at, truetype.variationItem(0, 3), null);
